# Development Rules for Cursor AI - Dashly App Implementation

## Core Development Principles

1. *Production-Ready Mindset*: Develop every feature as if it's going into production immediately. Approach the codebase with the mentality that it will be shipped to real users, not as a prototype or demo.

2. *Complete Implementation*: Every feature mentioned in the requirements must be fully implemented. No placeholder code or partial implementations.

3. *Zero TODOs*: Do not leave any "TODO" comments, "Implement later" notes, or similar placeholders in the code. If a feature is in the requirements, implement it completely.

4. *No Hypothetical Code*: Avoid comments like "in a real app," "in production this would," or "normally you would." Implement the actual production-ready solution instead.

5. *End-to-End Feature Completion*: Each feature should be implemented from UI to business logic to data persistence. No feature should be left incomplete at any layer of the application.

## Specific Implementation Guidelines

6. *Error Handling*: Implement comprehensive error handling for all network requests, user inputs, and system interactions. Do not leave edge cases unhandled.

7. *Loading States*: Implement proper loading states for all async operations. Users should always know when the app is working in the background.

8. *Validation*: Include complete input validation for all user-entered data with appropriate error messages.

9. *Persistence*: Implement proper data persistence where required - don't leave any persistence logic as placeholders.

10. *Authentication*: Fully implement the authentication flow including edge cases (session expiry, network issues during authentication, etc.).

11. *Navigation*: Implement complete navigation flows with proper state management. Don't leave navigation transitions or state handling incomplete.

12. *API Integration*: Implement all Firebase/backend interactions completely. No mock data that "would be replaced in production."

13. *Offline Support*: Implement the specified offline functionality completely rather than leaving comments about what "could be done" for offline support.

14. *Localization*: Set up the full localization infrastructure for the specified languages (English, Hindi, Marathi), not just placeholders.

15. *Code Organization*: Follow proper code organization patterns. No comments about "in a larger app, this would be in a separate file/module."

## Quality Assurance Requirements

16. *Code Commenting*: Include meaningful comments explaining "why" certain approaches were taken, not just placeholders for future work.

17. *Consistent Styling*: Maintain consistent code style and UI design throughout the entire application.

18. *Performance Optimization*: Implement all performance optimizations mentioned in requirements. Don't leave performance improvements as notes for future work.

19. *Security Implementation*: Fully implement all security requirements including input sanitization, secure storage, and API security.

20. *Image Handling*: Implement proper image loading, caching, and optimization - don't leave image handling as basic implementations with notes about improvements.

## Final Delivery Standards

21. *Complete Dependencies*: Include all necessary dependencies in pubspec.yaml with specific versions, not ranges or notes about "add appropriate version."

22. *Configuration Files*: Provide all necessary configuration files without placeholders (Google Services configs, Firebase configs, etc.).

23. *Documentation*: Provide comprehensive documentation for any complex logic or systems, not placeholders stating "this would be documented."

24. *State Management*: Fully implement the state management solution for all features, not simplified versions with notes about improvements.

25. *Testability*: Write code that can be tested, even if actual tests aren't included in the MVP. Don't leave notes about "making this testable later."

Remember: This is a production application that will be deployed to real users in Tier 3/4 Indian cities. Every line of code must reflect that reality and provide a complete, robust user experience.
